1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,000
I want to talk today about behavioral finance or about

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:08,760
psychology and finance.

3
00:00:08,760 --> 00:00:13,200
This is a longstanding interest of mine.

4
00:00:13,200 --> 00:00:17,240
I've been involved with it for over 20 years.

5
00:00:17,240 --> 00:00:21,240
It's not really emphasized in your textbook.

6
00:00:21,240 --> 00:00:23,980
Fibozzi and his co-authors talk

7
00:00:23,980 --> 00:00:26,720
about a lot of things in

8
00:00:26,720 --> 00:00:30,480
the financial world, but not about the underlying human

9
00:00:30,480 --> 00:00:31,720
behavior.

10
00:00:31,720 --> 00:00:37,480
Behavioral finance or behavioral economics more

11
00:00:37,480 --> 00:00:41,480
broadly is a kind of revolution that has occurred

12
00:00:41,480 --> 00:00:44,980
in finance and economics over

13
00:00:44,980 --> 00:00:48,480
the last 20 or 30 years.

14
00:00:48,480 --> 00:00:51,480
And it remains somewhat controversial.

15
00:00:51,480 --> 00:00:55,480
I don't quite fully understand the concept of

16
00:00:55,480 --> 00:00:56,480
financial finance.

17
00:00:56,480 --> 00:00:57,980
I don't quite fully understand

18
00:00:57,980 --> 00:00:59,480
why it is that people polarize

19
00:00:59,480 --> 00:01:00,480
as much as they do.

20
00:01:00,480 --> 00:01:03,720
But some people don't like this.

21
00:01:03,720 --> 00:01:08,479
Other people, I think, we're coming along to be the

22
00:01:08,479 --> 00:01:09,720
majority, I think.

23
00:01:09,720 --> 00:01:12,479
People are now regarding behavioral finance as an

24
00:01:12,479 --> 00:01:16,140
important element of finance.

25
00:01:16,140 --> 00:01:22,240
And so, the real problem is that people are complex.

26
00:01:22,240 --> 00:01:26,479
And our financial institutions, as I've emphasized, are

27
00:01:26,480 --> 00:01:28,480
designed for real people.

28
00:01:28,480 --> 00:01:32,480
And their functioning depends on

29
00:01:32,480 --> 00:01:36,480
the behavior of real people.

30
00:01:36,480 --> 00:01:40,480
And it's not as simple.

31
00:01:40,480 --> 00:01:44,480
Another revolution that's occurring parallel, of bigger

32
00:01:44,480 --> 00:01:46,980
significance, is a revolution

33
00:01:46,980 --> 00:01:49,480
in neuroscience about the human

34
00:01:49,480 --> 00:01:50,480
brain.

35
00:01:50,480 --> 00:01:55,480
And the human brain is a very complicated organ.

36
00:01:55,480 --> 00:02:02,480
And economists have liked to invoke the principle of

37
00:02:02,480 --> 00:02:07,480
rationality as an underlying component of their theory.

38
00:02:07,480 --> 00:02:09,479
And that has been useful.

39
00:02:09,479 --> 00:02:14,480
But it's of limited use, because people aren't rational.

40
00:02:14,480 --> 00:02:16,480
They're often rational.

41
00:02:16,480 --> 00:02:18,480
They're not completely rational.

42
00:02:18,480 --> 00:02:21,480
And very often, people behave stupidly.

43
00:02:21,480 --> 00:02:23,480
I'll put it that way.

44
00:02:23,480 --> 00:02:24,480
That includes everyone, including me.

45
00:02:24,480 --> 00:02:31,480
Because we're human, and we have limits.

46
00:02:31,480 --> 00:02:35,480
And that encourages, one thing

47
00:02:35,480 --> 00:02:39,480
about the human species is that

48
00:02:39,480 --> 00:02:41,480
we are aware of other

49
00:02:41,480 --> 00:02:43,480
people's weaknesses and have an impulse

50
00:02:43,480 --> 00:02:45,480
to exploit them.

51
00:02:45,480 --> 00:02:49,480
So, when you see other people behaving stupidly, sometimes

52
00:02:49,480 --> 00:02:52,480
you think, maybe I can turn this to my advantage.

53
00:02:52,480 --> 00:02:53,480
Okay?

54
00:02:53,480 --> 00:03:00,480
And that becomes a problem.

55
00:03:00,480 --> 00:03:02,980
The history of humankind is

56
00:03:02,980 --> 00:03:05,480
a history of exploitation of one

57
00:03:05,480 --> 00:03:08,480
person by another.

58
00:03:08,480 --> 00:03:10,980
Not entirely, but I'm saying it

59
00:03:10,980 --> 00:03:13,480
has that as an important element.

60
00:03:13,480 --> 00:03:16,480
So, I'm going to talk about these human failings.

61
00:03:16,480 --> 00:03:18,480
It's not to say that people are stupid.

62
00:03:18,480 --> 00:03:20,480
I'm just saying they're people, right?

63
00:03:20,480 --> 00:03:21,480
And we're all imperfect.

64
00:03:21,480 --> 00:03:22,480
We're smart in some dimensions.

65
00:03:22,480 --> 00:03:25,980
And we can be very smart,

66
00:03:25,980 --> 00:03:29,480
but we can also make important mistakes.

67
00:03:29,480 --> 00:03:32,980
But before I start, I wanted to

68
00:03:32,980 --> 00:03:36,480
try to put this into a perspective.

69
00:03:36,480 --> 00:03:37,980
Because maybe I'll return to this

70
00:03:37,980 --> 00:03:39,480
at the end of the lecture.

71
00:03:39,480 --> 00:03:45,480
But I wanted to start on an upbeat note.

72
00:03:45,480 --> 00:03:47,480
I'm going to talk about all kinds of human errors.

73
00:03:47,480 --> 00:03:50,480
But I wanted to start on an upbeat note.

74
00:03:50,480 --> 00:03:53,480
I'm going to start on an upbeat note.

75
00:03:53,480 --> 00:03:57,980
That the business world generally

76
00:03:57,980 --> 00:04:02,480
doesn't exploit people terribly, I believe.

77
00:04:02,480 --> 00:04:08,480
That very characteristically successful

78
00:04:08,480 --> 00:04:14,480
businesses in finance and elsewhere

79
00:04:14,480 --> 00:04:16,980
consider their long-term advantage

80
00:04:16,980 --> 00:04:19,480
and the reputation they have.

81
00:04:20,480 --> 00:04:26,480
So, doing something that is blatantly exploitative of

82
00:04:26,480 --> 00:04:32,480
human weaknesses will work against their long-term advantage.

83
00:04:32,480 --> 00:04:37,480
And so, we don't see, you'll see a lot of human failings, but

84
00:04:37,480 --> 00:04:42,480
we don't see people cashing in on them as often as you'd think.

85
00:04:42,480 --> 00:04:45,980
And beyond that, I want to emphasize also

86
00:04:45,980 --> 00:04:49,480
that another aspect of human behavior is morality.

87
00:04:50,480 --> 00:04:53,980
Evolutionary biologists think that this

88
00:04:53,980 --> 00:04:57,480
evolved along with our other traits.

89
00:04:57,480 --> 00:05:00,480
That we have an impulse to be moral.

90
00:05:00,480 --> 00:05:04,480
And so, in the long run, you might not

91
00:05:04,480 --> 00:05:08,480
really gain so much satisfaction from exploiting other people's mistakes.

92
00:05:08,480 --> 00:05:14,480
And so, you don't necessarily do that.

93
00:05:14,480 --> 00:05:18,480
So, that's why we'll have a lot of weaknesses outlined.

94
00:05:18,480 --> 00:05:18,980
And we won't see, you know, we won't see

95
00:05:18,980 --> 00:05:19,480
a lot of people who are not good at it.

96
00:05:19,480 --> 00:05:19,980
And we won't see a lot of

97
00:05:19,980 --> 00:05:20,480
people who are not good at it.

98
00:05:20,480 --> 00:05:21,480
But we'll see a lot of

99
00:05:21,480 --> 00:05:22,480
people who are not good at it.

100
00:05:22,480 --> 00:05:24,980
And so, in the long run, we'll certainly see

101
00:05:24,980 --> 00:05:27,480
a significant or serious exploitation of them as characteristic.

102
00:05:27,480 --> 00:05:30,980
Now, as you know, I have chapters

103
00:05:30,980 --> 00:05:34,480
from my forthcoming book assigned for this course.

104
00:05:34,480 --> 00:05:35,980
And I look back on what

105
00:05:35,980 --> 00:05:37,480
I put up for you to read.

106
00:05:37,480 --> 00:05:41,480
And I keep thinking, gee, this really wasn't ready.

107
00:05:41,480 --> 00:05:44,980
So, I had a chapter for this

108
00:05:44,980 --> 00:05:48,480
section of the reading list about behavioral finance.

109
00:05:48,480 --> 00:05:50,040
I didn't really get it right.

110
00:05:50,040 --> 00:05:52,720
I know what I was trying to say.

111
00:05:52,720 --> 00:05:54,830
What I start out in

112
00:05:54,830 --> 00:05:56,940
that chapter is talking about Adam

113
00:05:56,940 --> 00:06:03,620
Smith and his book, The Theory of Moral Sentiments.

114
00:06:03,620 --> 00:06:06,980
Now, just to remind you,

115
00:06:06,980 --> 00:06:10,340
Adam Smith was a professor in

116
00:06:10,340 --> 00:06:17,060
Scotland that in 1759, he was a professor of moral

117
00:06:17,060 --> 00:06:19,600
philosophy, because there were no professors of

118
00:06:19,600 --> 00:06:22,720
economics in those days.

119
00:06:22,720 --> 00:06:24,820
And he wrote in 1759, maybe

120
00:06:24,820 --> 00:06:26,920
I should write some of this on

121
00:06:26,920 --> 00:06:28,180
the board.

122
00:06:34,440 --> 00:06:37,780
He's probably the most important figure in the

123
00:06:37,780 --> 00:06:40,980
history of economic thought.

124
00:06:40,980 --> 00:06:46,680
So, in 1759, he wrote his Theory of Moral Sentiments.

125
00:06:47,060 --> 00:06:50,280
And in 1776, he wrote

126
00:06:50,280 --> 00:06:53,500
the more famous book, Wealth of

127
00:06:53,500 --> 00:06:54,700
Nations.

128
00:06:58,700 --> 00:07:03,740
So, this is the Theory of Moral Sentiments.

129
00:07:03,740 --> 00:07:11,520
The Wealth of Nations is considered the first real

130
00:07:11,520 --> 00:07:12,820
treatise on economics.

131
00:07:12,820 --> 00:07:15,940
And it's a wonderful book, and it's still very readable

132
00:07:15,940 --> 00:07:16,360
today.

133
00:07:16,360 --> 00:07:16,840
Thank you.

134
00:07:16,840 --> 00:07:17,900
I'd like to thank you for

135
00:07:17,900 --> 00:07:18,960
taking the time to read it

136
00:07:18,960 --> 00:07:19,440
today.

137
00:07:19,440 --> 00:07:22,350
His Theory of Moral Sentiments is

138
00:07:22,350 --> 00:07:25,260
not so widely read, but it's

139
00:07:25,260 --> 00:07:26,640
not really economics.

140
00:07:26,640 --> 00:07:30,859
It's a book about psychology and morality.

141
00:07:30,859 --> 00:07:36,620
And I find it very good, even 250 years later.

142
00:07:36,620 --> 00:07:38,450
He went through many editions

143
00:07:38,450 --> 00:07:40,280
on this book, because maybe he

144
00:07:40,280 --> 00:07:42,440
thought it was his most important work.

145
00:07:42,440 --> 00:07:46,140
But the book starts out about selfishness and altruism.

146
00:07:46,140 --> 00:07:51,960
And the real question, which he defines economics, is, are

147
00:07:51,960 --> 00:07:54,260
people really completely selfish?

148
00:07:54,260 --> 00:07:58,219
Sometimes it seems that way, that their presumed

149
00:07:58,219 --> 00:08:01,700
benevolence is just an artifice for their own benefit.

150
00:08:05,020 --> 00:08:06,339
But he wonders, how does

151
00:08:06,339 --> 00:08:07,659
an economy work if everyone is

152
00:08:07,659 --> 00:08:08,760
totally selfish?

153
00:08:08,760 --> 00:08:11,700
And he ends up concluding that they're not.

154
00:08:11,700 --> 00:08:13,220
I thought it was very

155
00:08:13,220 --> 00:08:14,740
interesting the way he put it.

156
00:08:14,740 --> 00:08:16,190
The thing he emphasized right at

157
00:08:16,190 --> 00:08:17,640
the beginning of the book is

158
00:08:17,640 --> 00:08:23,100
that people inherently love praise.

159
00:08:23,100 --> 00:08:27,120
We crave the approval of other people.

160
00:08:27,120 --> 00:08:33,059
And so praise is a fundamental human desire.

161
00:08:33,059 --> 00:08:34,559
But then he reflected on it,

162
00:08:34,559 --> 00:08:36,059
and he said, do people really

163
00:08:36,059 --> 00:08:38,820
want praise itself, or is

164
00:08:38,820 --> 00:08:41,580
it something else that they want?

165
00:08:41,580 --> 00:08:43,260
And he said, well, think of it this way.

166
00:08:43,260 --> 00:08:44,720
Suppose you have a

167
00:08:44,740 --> 00:08:46,740
lot of people who are doing it.

168
00:08:46,740 --> 00:08:48,270
And you find that people made

169
00:08:48,270 --> 00:08:49,800
a big mistake and thought that

170
00:08:49,800 --> 00:08:51,800
you had accomplished something.

171
00:08:51,800 --> 00:08:53,300
But there was a mix-up.

172
00:08:53,300 --> 00:08:55,980
It was really somebody else who did it, not you.

173
00:08:55,980 --> 00:08:58,180
And it's just a complete mistake.

174
00:08:58,180 --> 00:08:59,800
You had nothing to do with it.

175
00:08:59,800 --> 00:09:02,500
But you find lots of people praising you.

176
00:09:02,500 --> 00:09:05,500
Would that really be pleasurable?

177
00:09:05,500 --> 00:09:07,150
And suppose you even know that

178
00:09:07,150 --> 00:09:08,800
they'll never find out that I

179
00:09:08,800 --> 00:09:10,000
didn't do it.

180
00:09:10,000 --> 00:09:12,100
Well, Smith said, it probably isn't.

181
00:09:12,100 --> 00:09:13,500
I mean, think about it.

182
00:09:13,500 --> 00:09:15,660
I'm getting all this praise, but

183
00:09:15,660 --> 00:09:17,820
I know I don't deserve it.

184
00:09:17,820 --> 00:09:20,040
So I don't enjoy it.

185
00:09:20,040 --> 00:09:21,580
And then he went on

186
00:09:21,580 --> 00:09:23,120
to say that, especially among people

187
00:09:23,120 --> 00:09:26,940
who are more mature, he says, more mature people, not

188
00:09:26,940 --> 00:09:28,800
everyone makes this step.

189
00:09:28,800 --> 00:09:33,500
But he says, adults, normal, mature adults, make a

190
00:09:33,500 --> 00:09:38,020
transition from a desire for praise to a desire for

191
00:09:38,020 --> 00:09:40,440
praiseworthiness.

192
00:09:40,440 --> 00:09:43,500
I want to know that I am the kind of

193
00:09:43,500 --> 00:09:45,440
person who will be praised, and

194
00:09:45,440 --> 00:09:47,380
I don't need to get the

195
00:09:47,380 --> 00:09:48,420
praise.

196
00:09:48,420 --> 00:09:51,780
And he said, it's that tendency, ultimately, which

197
00:09:51,780 --> 00:09:53,820
makes an economy work.

198
00:09:53,820 --> 00:09:58,140
That people don't care just about praise.

199
00:09:58,140 --> 00:10:00,580
He gives an example of mathematicians.

200
00:10:00,580 --> 00:10:03,580
And he said he's known many mathematicians in his life.

201
00:10:03,580 --> 00:10:06,300
And he finds that they're almost all obscure.

202
00:10:06,300 --> 00:10:08,700
The public doesn't know about mathematicians.

203
00:10:08,700 --> 00:10:12,120
They couldn't explain to the public what they do.

204
00:10:12,120 --> 00:10:12,820
And they don't seem to care at all.

205
00:10:12,820 --> 00:10:12,940
They don't seem to care about

206
00:10:12,940 --> 00:10:13,060
the fact that they're not doing

207
00:10:13,060 --> 00:10:13,180
anything.

208
00:10:13,180 --> 00:10:13,420
They're doing something.

209
00:10:13,420 --> 00:10:13,480
They're doing something.

210
00:10:13,500 --> 00:10:16,460
And they don't seem to care at all.

211
00:10:16,460 --> 00:10:20,420
Because they know the public doesn't appreciate mathematics.

212
00:10:20,420 --> 00:10:23,880
And so, there's a few mathematician friends who

213
00:10:23,880 --> 00:10:27,320
understand what they do and may praise them.

214
00:10:27,320 --> 00:10:30,400
But ultimately, a mathematician can sometimes do the work

215
00:10:30,400 --> 00:10:34,660
completely unknown, writing.

216
00:10:34,660 --> 00:10:40,920
And it's the praiseworthiness that drives these people.

217
00:10:40,920 --> 00:10:42,170
So, I think that you may

218
00:10:42,170 --> 00:10:43,420
think that I'm being too idealistic.

219
00:10:43,420 --> 00:10:44,800
When I say this.

220
00:10:44,800 --> 00:10:48,340
But I think that the finance profession, this is

221
00:10:48,340 --> 00:10:51,880
what I was trying to say in that chapter.

222
00:10:51,880 --> 00:10:55,530
That the finance profession, like other

223
00:10:55,530 --> 00:10:59,180
mature professions, is really dominated, although

224
00:10:59,180 --> 00:11:01,900
there's a lot of funny things that happen,

225
00:11:01,900 --> 00:11:04,620
is really dominated by people who have reached

226
00:11:04,620 --> 00:11:07,980
this desire for praiseworthiness.

227
00:11:07,980 --> 00:11:10,360
And so, you're not going to exploit

228
00:11:10,360 --> 00:11:12,740
people extravagantly, just because why would I do

229
00:11:12,740 --> 00:11:13,339
that?

230
00:11:13,340 --> 00:11:14,340
That's not a good thing.

231
00:11:14,340 --> 00:11:17,540
I wouldn't feel good about it.

232
00:11:17,540 --> 00:11:18,540
Though some people will.

233
00:11:18,540 --> 00:11:23,330
Now, I wanted to also mention, not

234
00:11:23,330 --> 00:11:28,120
everyone reaches this mature state that Adam Smith

235
00:11:28,120 --> 00:11:29,660
describes.

236
00:11:29,660 --> 00:11:32,340
And that's one of the complexities of human society.

237
00:11:32,340 --> 00:11:34,930
And I think that the finance profession

238
00:11:34,930 --> 00:11:37,520
has a problem with other kinds of people.

239
00:11:37,520 --> 00:11:40,340
Now, there's a whole branch of

240
00:11:40,340 --> 00:11:43,160
psychology called personality psychology that categorizes people

241
00:11:43,160 --> 00:11:45,100
by their personality.

242
00:11:45,100 --> 00:11:48,260
And we're not all the same.

243
00:11:48,260 --> 00:11:51,669
In our society, we have

244
00:11:51,669 --> 00:11:55,079
many different kinds of personalities.

245
00:11:55,079 --> 00:11:58,489
A successful society promotes people

246
00:11:58,489 --> 00:12:01,900
up who have the praiseworthiness desire.

247
00:12:01,900 --> 00:12:04,840
We try to recognize them and we try

248
00:12:04,840 --> 00:12:07,780
to put people of character into important positions.

249
00:12:07,780 --> 00:12:10,360
With limit, not complete success.

250
00:12:10,360 --> 00:12:12,480
But I wanted to just briefly talk about.

251
00:12:12,480 --> 00:12:15,720
This is a lecture on psychology.

252
00:12:15,720 --> 00:12:20,080
And I wanted to talk about other personality types.

253
00:12:20,080 --> 00:12:22,360
And I was going to use

254
00:12:22,360 --> 00:12:24,640
a book called The Diagnostic and

255
00:12:24,640 --> 00:12:29,920
Statistical Manual, edition four, published by the

256
00:12:29,920 --> 00:12:32,640
American Psychiatric Association.

257
00:12:32,640 --> 00:12:37,360
They're coming out with a fifth edition in 2014.

258
00:12:37,360 --> 00:12:39,280
DSM-IV is kind of a

259
00:12:39,280 --> 00:12:41,200
household word around my house, because

260
00:12:41,200 --> 00:12:46,320
I'm a psychiatrist, and my wife is a psychologist.

261
00:12:46,320 --> 00:12:49,440
DSM-IV is actually controversial among

262
00:12:49,440 --> 00:12:51,440
psychiatrists, because it's too cut

263
00:12:51,440 --> 00:12:53,440
and dried for some of them.

264
00:12:53,440 --> 00:12:57,840
What it tries to do is identify mental illnesses and

265
00:12:57,840 --> 00:13:02,800
personality types in a quantifiable, reproducible way,

266
00:13:02,800 --> 00:13:05,200
so that we can define, who

267
00:13:05,200 --> 00:13:07,600
has this mental illness, or who has

268
00:13:07,600 --> 00:13:11,120
this personality type.

269
00:13:11,120 --> 00:13:12,400
And it's a checklist.

270
00:13:12,400 --> 00:13:15,360
And it says, the patient

271
00:13:15,360 --> 00:13:18,320
must have exhibited at least three

272
00:13:18,320 --> 00:13:21,040
of the following five behaviors.

273
00:13:21,040 --> 00:13:23,520
And then there'll be another checklist.

274
00:13:23,520 --> 00:13:24,720
And so, you keep scoring.

275
00:13:24,720 --> 00:13:29,680
You can actually diagnose personality disorder.

276
00:13:29,680 --> 00:13:30,880
I'm just going to mention one.

277
00:13:30,880 --> 00:13:34,160
They have many personality disorders.

278
00:13:34,160 --> 00:13:39,680
One of them is called APD, called Antisocial Personality

279
00:13:39,680 --> 00:13:40,080
Disorder.

280
00:13:41,120 --> 00:13:42,240
And I don't know if you have

281
00:13:42,240 --> 00:13:43,360
a checklist, but just to give you

282
00:13:43,360 --> 00:13:44,720
a sense.

283
00:13:44,720 --> 00:13:50,720
Oh, and the Antisocial Personality Disorder is called

284
00:13:50,720 --> 00:13:52,800
psychopathy.

285
00:13:52,800 --> 00:13:56,720
Or one kind of APD is psychopathy.

286
00:13:56,720 --> 00:14:00,000
Another one is called sociopathy.

287
00:14:00,000 --> 00:14:03,120
And I don't know, there's a huge literature on these.

288
00:14:03,120 --> 00:14:07,040
But according to DSM-IV, 3%

289
00:14:07,040 --> 00:14:10,960
of the male population in the

290
00:14:10,960 --> 00:14:18,560
female population is APD.

291
00:14:18,560 --> 00:14:22,640
And 1% of the female population.

292
00:14:22,640 --> 00:14:26,640
A simple definition for APD is a jerk.

293
00:14:26,640 --> 00:14:32,960
There are more male jerks than female jerks, apparently.

294
00:14:32,960 --> 00:14:36,960
According to their, this is all quantifiable and done.

295
00:14:36,960 --> 00:14:40,800
But what is an antisocial personality disorder?

296
00:14:40,800 --> 00:14:42,800
It's a very common characteristic.

297
00:14:42,800 --> 00:14:44,800
Lack of remorse.

298
00:14:44,800 --> 00:14:46,800
Frequent lying.

299
00:14:46,800 --> 00:14:48,800
Lack of empathy.

300
00:14:48,800 --> 00:14:50,800
Superficial charm.

301
00:14:50,800 --> 00:14:52,800
Shallowed emotions.

302
00:14:52,800 --> 00:14:54,800
Distorted sense of self.

303
00:14:54,800 --> 00:14:56,800
Constant search for new sensations.

304
00:14:56,800 --> 00:14:58,800
Have you met someone like that?

305
00:14:58,800 --> 00:15:04,800
You probably have, because that's 3% of the population.

306
00:15:04,800 --> 00:15:06,800
I'm not anti-male when I point

307
00:15:06,800 --> 00:15:08,800
out there are three times as

308
00:15:08,800 --> 00:15:10,800
many jerks among males as females.

309
00:15:10,800 --> 00:15:12,800
Females have characteristically

310
00:15:12,800 --> 00:15:14,800
different personality disorders.

311
00:15:14,800 --> 00:15:16,800
And you can look down the list.

312
00:15:16,800 --> 00:15:18,800
So, it's much more than 3%

313
00:15:18,800 --> 00:15:20,800
of the population that would be

314
00:15:20,800 --> 00:15:22,800
diagnosed with one or the other.

315
00:15:22,800 --> 00:15:24,800
So, you know what an APD person is?

316
00:15:24,800 --> 00:15:27,800
Manipulative, feigns affectionate or warm

317
00:15:27,800 --> 00:15:30,800
feelings, but doesn't feel them.

318
00:15:30,800 --> 00:15:34,800
And is trying to deceive you.

319
00:15:34,800 --> 00:15:36,800
I encountered one.

320
00:15:36,800 --> 00:15:38,800
Once a student came to my office

321
00:15:38,800 --> 00:15:40,800
and asked to sign up as my

322
00:15:40,800 --> 00:15:42,800
research assistant.

323
00:15:42,800 --> 00:15:44,800
And I was talking to him.

324
00:15:44,800 --> 00:15:46,800
I thought, well, maybe, you know.

325
00:15:46,800 --> 00:15:48,800
I said, come back and talk to me.

326
00:15:48,800 --> 00:15:49,800
Later on, I read about

327
00:15:49,800 --> 00:15:50,800
him in the Yale Daily News.

328
00:15:50,800 --> 00:15:52,800
He was an imposter student.

329
00:15:52,800 --> 00:15:54,800
He was not a Yale student.

330
00:15:54,800 --> 00:15:56,800
And he had been around to other university campuses.

331
00:15:56,800 --> 00:15:58,800
He was an imposter at like three different campuses.

332
00:15:58,800 --> 00:16:00,800
So, there's something wrong with this person, right?

333
00:16:00,800 --> 00:16:02,800
Kind of made me feel.

334
00:16:02,800 --> 00:16:03,800
Then he came later and

335
00:16:03,800 --> 00:16:04,800
asked me for a recommendation letter.

336
00:16:04,800 --> 00:16:06,800
I couldn't believe it.

337
00:16:06,800 --> 00:16:08,800
After I read about him in the Yale Daily News.

338
00:16:08,800 --> 00:16:10,800
I said, well, I'm not a Yale student.

339
00:16:10,800 --> 00:16:12,800
I could have just seen him on the news.

340
00:16:12,800 --> 00:16:14,800
But those are, this is extreme.

341
00:16:14,800 --> 00:16:16,800
Right?

342
00:16:16,800 --> 00:16:19,800
And so, incidentally, someone did a study

343
00:16:19,800 --> 00:16:22,800
of APD by going to a prison and

344
00:16:22,800 --> 00:16:26,800
categorizing the inmates.

345
00:16:26,800 --> 00:16:28,800
Using DSM-IV standards.

346
00:16:28,800 --> 00:16:32,800
And they found that 40% of the prisoners had APD.

347
00:16:32,800 --> 00:16:34,800
Also, neuroscience people have found

348
00:16:34,800 --> 00:16:36,800
that there are differences in the

349
00:16:36,800 --> 00:16:38,800
prefrontal cortex.

350
00:16:38,800 --> 00:16:41,560
cortex that are correlated with APD.

351
00:16:41,560 --> 00:16:43,400
So, it seems to be, it's

352
00:16:43,400 --> 00:16:45,240
a problem we have in our society

353
00:16:45,240 --> 00:16:47,959
that some people have a brain structure that's a little

354
00:16:47,959 --> 00:16:50,359
different, and it may make

355
00:16:50,359 --> 00:16:52,760
it difficult for them to behave

356
00:16:52,760 --> 00:16:54,240
in a good way.

357
00:16:54,240 --> 00:17:02,060
So, we're learning more and more about neuroscience.

358
00:17:02,060 --> 00:17:06,119
And it's interesting to me that Adam Smith's book still

359
00:17:06,119 --> 00:17:07,839
rings true, though.

360
00:17:07,839 --> 00:17:08,839
It's a very interesting book.

361
00:17:08,839 --> 00:17:10,839
And it's some basic common sense that we all learn.

362
00:17:10,839 --> 00:17:11,839
You have to judge people,

363
00:17:11,839 --> 00:17:12,839
and you have to learn their

364
00:17:12,839 --> 00:17:14,839
character, and you have to be

365
00:17:14,839 --> 00:17:16,839
a person of character, for in

366
00:17:16,839 --> 00:17:19,839
the long run, that's what you want.

367
00:17:19,839 --> 00:17:21,839
It gives you what you want in life.

368
00:17:31,840 --> 00:17:33,340
So, I'm going to talk about,

369
00:17:33,340 --> 00:17:34,840
I'm going to turn now to, oh,

370
00:17:34,840 --> 00:17:36,340
another thing I wanted to

371
00:17:36,340 --> 00:17:37,840
say at the beginning is that

372
00:17:37,840 --> 00:17:38,840
we're all manipulable.

373
00:17:38,840 --> 00:17:41,840
Unfortunately true.

374
00:17:41,840 --> 00:17:45,340
And unfortunately, we live in

375
00:17:45,340 --> 00:17:48,840
a world where it's hard to

376
00:17:48,840 --> 00:17:54,840
avoid manipulating people at all, because we have a free

377
00:17:54,840 --> 00:17:58,840
enterprise system that encourages competition.

378
00:17:58,840 --> 00:18:02,840
And if the competition is manipulating people, how do you

379
00:18:02,840 --> 00:18:05,840
completely stay clear of that?

380
00:18:05,840 --> 00:18:06,840
And I think this is one

381
00:18:06,840 --> 00:18:07,840
of the contradictions of our society.

382
00:18:07,840 --> 00:18:08,840
And I think that's the key.

383
00:18:08,840 --> 00:18:10,840
And a very simple and

384
00:18:10,840 --> 00:18:12,840
obvious manipulation is, they'll put a

385
00:18:12,840 --> 00:18:18,840
price on some item they're selling, like $9.99.

386
00:18:18,840 --> 00:18:20,340
So, you ask, well, why

387
00:18:20,340 --> 00:18:21,840
didn't they just say $10, right?

388
00:18:21,840 --> 00:18:23,840
Well, you know why they didn't.

389
00:18:23,840 --> 00:18:25,840
It's called pricing points in marketing.

390
00:18:25,840 --> 00:18:31,840
Because $9.99 sounds a lot less psychologically than $10.

391
00:18:31,840 --> 00:18:33,840
So, everyone does it.

392
00:18:33,840 --> 00:18:35,840
Almost everyone does it.

393
00:18:35,840 --> 00:18:36,840
But, yeah.

394
00:18:36,840 --> 00:18:38,840
And I think that's the key.

395
00:18:38,840 --> 00:18:40,840
And I think that's the key.

396
00:18:40,840 --> 00:18:42,840
And I think that's the key.

397
00:18:42,840 --> 00:18:44,840
Is that bad?

398
00:18:44,840 --> 00:18:45,840
Well, it's bad in a way.

399
00:18:45,840 --> 00:18:47,840
It's manipulative, isn't it?

400
00:18:47,840 --> 00:18:48,840
I mean, I'm annoyed by it.

401
00:18:48,840 --> 00:18:50,840
Maybe you are, too.

402
00:18:50,840 --> 00:18:52,840
But if you were in

403
00:18:52,840 --> 00:18:54,840
business, would you do that, too?

404
00:18:54,840 --> 00:18:56,840
You might feel that you have to.

405
00:18:56,840 --> 00:18:59,840
And it's a harmless sort of manipulation, right?

406
00:18:59,840 --> 00:19:01,840
It's not hurting anyone, really.

407
00:19:01,840 --> 00:19:03,340
They may be buying a

408
00:19:03,340 --> 00:19:04,840
little bit more than they want.

409
00:19:04,840 --> 00:19:05,840
But you see, that's the

410
00:19:05,840 --> 00:19:06,840
kind of thing that comes in.

411
00:19:06,840 --> 00:19:10,340
And in looking at financial institutions,

412
00:19:10,340 --> 00:19:13,840
they're often manipulative in that sense.

413
00:19:13,840 --> 00:19:15,840
But I don't think that

414
00:19:15,840 --> 00:19:17,840
– it's similar to a politician.

415
00:19:17,840 --> 00:19:21,840
If you want to be a member of Congress

416
00:19:21,840 --> 00:19:25,840
or whatever, you can't say what you really believe, right?

417
00:19:25,840 --> 00:19:28,840
Because you won't get elected.

418
00:19:28,840 --> 00:19:31,840
You've got to kind of doctor

419
00:19:31,840 --> 00:19:34,840
your opinions to the public opinion.

420
00:19:34,840 --> 00:19:37,340
And so, otherwise, you won't have – but

421
00:19:37,340 --> 00:19:39,840
you might have a moral purpose underlying it all,

422
00:19:39,840 --> 00:19:41,840
because you want to get elected,

423
00:19:41,840 --> 00:19:43,840
so you can do good things.

424
00:19:43,840 --> 00:19:48,840
And so, you do end up saying things.

425
00:19:48,840 --> 00:19:51,840
So, it's hard to judge people, good or bad.

426
00:19:51,840 --> 00:19:54,840
It's an overall sense you get of someone's character,

427
00:19:54,840 --> 00:19:57,340
that people are doing things that

428
00:19:57,340 --> 00:19:59,840
appear somewhat manipulative and somewhat bad.

429
00:19:59,840 --> 00:20:01,340
But you get an overall

430
00:20:01,340 --> 00:20:02,840
sense of the person through time.

431
00:20:02,840 --> 00:20:03,840
And that's –

432
00:20:04,840 --> 00:20:07,840
and ultimately, our society, within

433
00:20:07,840 --> 00:20:10,840
limits, rewards people that show character

434
00:20:10,840 --> 00:20:14,840
through all the confusing details.

435
00:20:14,840 --> 00:20:16,840
Now, I wanted to move – that was my introduction.

436
00:20:16,840 --> 00:20:19,840
I wanted to move now to

437
00:20:19,840 --> 00:20:22,840
discussing some particular aspects of behavioral finance

438
00:20:22,840 --> 00:20:25,840
or more broadly, behavioral economics,

439
00:20:25,840 --> 00:20:32,840
and about human failings that are exploitable by somebody,

440
00:20:32,840 --> 00:20:42,840
and are somewhat exploited, but remain as –

441
00:20:42,840 --> 00:20:46,340
I wanted to start out with what's

442
00:20:46,340 --> 00:20:49,840
probably the most famous element of behavioral economics.

443
00:20:49,840 --> 00:20:56,840
It's prospect theory.

444
00:20:56,840 --> 00:21:00,840
And it was invented by two psychologists.

445
00:21:00,840 --> 00:21:01,840
Danny –

446
00:21:01,840 --> 00:21:09,840
or Daniel Kahneman and Amos Tversky.

447
00:21:09,840 --> 00:21:13,840
And in the late 20th century,

448
00:21:13,840 --> 00:21:17,840
they called it prospect theory, because it was

449
00:21:17,840 --> 00:21:21,840
a theory of how people form decisions about prospects.

450
00:21:21,840 --> 00:21:23,840
And a prospect is a gamble.

451
00:21:23,840 --> 00:21:27,840
It's about people's decisions under uncertainty.

452
00:21:27,840 --> 00:21:29,840
And in very simple terms,

453
00:21:29,840 --> 00:21:34,840
the prospect theory says –

454
00:21:34,840 --> 00:21:36,840
now, there's a huge literature on this,

455
00:21:36,840 --> 00:21:40,840
so I'm trying to give you

456
00:21:40,840 --> 00:21:44,840
a very quick description of it –

457
00:21:44,840 --> 00:21:47,840
that there's something called a value function,

458
00:21:47,840 --> 00:21:53,840
which represents how people value things.

459
00:21:53,840 --> 00:21:57,840
And there's a weighting function.

460
00:21:57,840 --> 00:22:00,840
And it's the two parts,

461
00:22:00,840 --> 00:22:05,840
which shows how people infer –

462
00:22:05,840 --> 00:22:08,840
how they deal with probabilities.

463
00:22:08,840 --> 00:22:11,340
Okay, and I'll just show

464
00:22:11,340 --> 00:22:13,840
simply what Kahneman and Tversky says.

465
00:22:13,840 --> 00:22:15,840
I'll draw a picture of the

466
00:22:15,840 --> 00:22:17,840
value function and the weighting function.

467
00:22:17,840 --> 00:22:19,840
And this will be very quick,

468
00:22:19,840 --> 00:22:22,840
and you'd have to read more.

469
00:22:22,840 --> 00:22:26,840
But the way people value gains or losses –

470
00:22:27,840 --> 00:22:30,840
let me see, how do I –

471
00:22:30,840 --> 00:22:36,840
I better draw the line in the middle.

472
00:22:36,840 --> 00:22:39,840
Okay, and these we're talking about financial gains.

473
00:22:39,840 --> 00:22:42,840
So, these are gains.

474
00:22:42,840 --> 00:22:44,840
And this is zero.

475
00:22:44,840 --> 00:22:46,840
And this is losses.

476
00:22:46,840 --> 00:22:47,840
Well, negative.

477
00:22:47,840 --> 00:22:50,340
What I have on the horizontal axis

478
00:22:50,340 --> 00:22:52,840
is wealth or money or something like that.

479
00:22:52,840 --> 00:22:54,840
Zero in the middle.

480
00:22:54,840 --> 00:22:55,840
Okay.

481
00:22:55,840 --> 00:22:56,840
And then,

482
00:22:56,840 --> 00:22:58,840
I have on this axis value,

483
00:22:58,840 --> 00:23:03,840
which is something like utility.

484
00:23:03,840 --> 00:23:05,840
I'll erase my zero.

485
00:23:05,840 --> 00:23:08,840
It doesn't get in the way.

486
00:23:08,840 --> 00:23:11,340
And what they find is that

487
00:23:11,340 --> 00:23:13,840
people's value has a funny shape.

488
00:23:13,840 --> 00:23:20,840
We don't weigh gains and losses linearly.

489
00:23:20,840 --> 00:23:22,840
In a positive quadrant,

490
00:23:22,840 --> 00:23:24,840
when we have positive gains,

491
00:23:24,840 --> 00:23:26,840
there's diminishing value,

492
00:23:26,840 --> 00:23:28,840
and we don't weigh losses.

493
00:23:28,840 --> 00:23:30,840
It doesn't ever slope down.

494
00:23:30,840 --> 00:23:33,840
It's concave down.

495
00:23:33,840 --> 00:23:38,840
Like diminishing marginal utility in economic theory.

496
00:23:38,840 --> 00:23:40,840
But for losses,

497
00:23:40,840 --> 00:23:42,840
it looks something like this.

498
00:23:42,840 --> 00:23:45,840
It's concave up.

499
00:23:45,840 --> 00:23:46,840
I'm exaggerating a little bit,

500
00:23:46,840 --> 00:23:52,840
but this is a diagram that Kahneman and Tversky wrote

501
00:23:52,840 --> 00:23:56,840
in their famous Econometrica article,

502
00:23:56,840 --> 00:23:58,840
a few years ago.

503
00:23:58,840 --> 00:24:02,840
So, there's diminishing marginal utility for gains,

504
00:24:02,840 --> 00:24:05,840
but there's the opposite.

505
00:24:05,840 --> 00:24:09,840
Well, we have concave up.

506
00:24:09,840 --> 00:24:10,840
And there's a kink.

507
00:24:10,840 --> 00:24:15,340
You note that the value function

508
00:24:15,340 --> 00:24:19,840
has a kink at the origin.

509
00:24:19,840 --> 00:24:21,840
So, what does this mean?

510
00:24:21,840 --> 00:24:22,840
It means,

511
00:24:22,840 --> 00:24:24,840
okay, first of all,

512
00:24:24,840 --> 00:24:25,840
this origin

513
00:24:25,840 --> 00:24:27,840
is a,

514
00:24:27,840 --> 00:24:28,840
what is,

515
00:24:28,840 --> 00:24:32,840
from what point do I estimate gains and losses?

516
00:24:32,840 --> 00:24:34,840
That's called the reference point.

517
00:24:34,840 --> 00:24:37,840
And it's psychological.

518
00:24:37,840 --> 00:24:39,840
And it's subject to manipulation.

519
00:24:39,840 --> 00:24:41,840
The reference point is the zero

520
00:24:41,840 --> 00:24:44,840
from which I measure things.

521
00:24:44,840 --> 00:24:45,840
The kink,

522
00:24:45,840 --> 00:24:47,840
so, first of all,

523
00:24:47,840 --> 00:24:48,840
it's,

524
00:24:48,840 --> 00:24:52,840
the reference point is probably today's wealth.

525
00:24:52,840 --> 00:24:54,840
But it can be something else,

526
00:24:54,840 --> 00:24:57,840
if people are manipulated

527
00:24:57,840 --> 00:25:01,840
by the way something is presented to them.

528
00:25:01,840 --> 00:25:04,840
Framing, according to Kahneman and Tversky,

529
00:25:04,840 --> 00:25:06,840
is presentation.

530
00:25:06,840 --> 00:25:10,840
So, I can give the same

531
00:25:10,840 --> 00:25:11,840
prospect to people,

532
00:25:11,840 --> 00:25:13,840
but word it in different ways

533
00:25:13,840 --> 00:25:16,840
that suggests a different reference point.

534
00:25:16,840 --> 00:25:19,840
And that will change people's behavior.

535
00:25:19,840 --> 00:25:21,840
So, you can manipulate people by

536
00:25:21,840 --> 00:25:23,840
describing something in different terms.

537
00:25:24,840 --> 00:25:27,840
By suggesting a different reference point.

538
00:25:27,840 --> 00:25:30,840
But typically, the reference point is today's wealth.

539
00:25:30,840 --> 00:25:32,840
The kink means

540
00:25:32,840 --> 00:25:34,840
that people are very conscious

541
00:25:34,840 --> 00:25:37,840
of little changes in their wealth.

542
00:25:37,840 --> 00:25:39,840
And they're spooked by them.

543
00:25:39,840 --> 00:25:40,840
I'm really afraid,

544
00:25:40,840 --> 00:25:42,840
because my value drops very rapidly,

545
00:25:42,840 --> 00:25:44,840
even for a small loss.

546
00:25:44,840 --> 00:25:46,840
So, if you were to say,

547
00:25:46,840 --> 00:25:50,840
lose $5 this morning,

548
00:25:50,840 --> 00:25:51,840
all right, you had it in your pocket

549
00:25:51,840 --> 00:25:53,840
and you lost it on the way to this lecture,

550
00:25:53,840 --> 00:25:56,840
